WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:02.490
- Hi, my name is Tanmay, Tanmay Bakshi.

00:00:02.490 --> 00:00:05.310
- How old are you?
- I'm 19 years old.

00:00:05.310 --> 00:00:06.990
I just turned 19 a couple days ago.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.573
- How can you be called a genius so early?

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.750
- I personally don't really
like to use that word.

00:00:12.750 --> 00:00:15.390
- What are some of your
biggest accomplishments?

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:17.670
- When I was nine I had
my first iOS app published

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:19.260
at the App Store, called tTables

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:21.780
to help kids learn their times tables.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:23.880
I found this documentary of IBM Watson.

00:00:23.880 --> 00:00:25.710
Not sure if you've heard
of Watson playing Jeopardy.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:28.110
So I started messing around
with the different IBM Watson

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.500
AI services, 'cause they made
those public on their cloud,

00:00:31.500 --> 00:00:34.110
and I actually found a bug
in one of their services

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:35.278
back when I was 11.

00:00:35.278 --> 00:00:36.540
- (laughing) That's so funny.

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:39.480
- I've authored four books
on the world of technology.

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:40.860
That's something I love to do.

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:43.260
Working on applications
all the way from analyzing

00:00:43.260 --> 00:00:45.090
your brainwaves to give people the ability

00:00:45.090 --> 00:00:47.760
to communicate artificially
to working with McDonald's

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.530
at IBM right now, using machine-learning

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:51.001
to take your orders in the drive through.

00:00:51.001 --> 00:00:51.834
- No wonder you're called a genius.

00:00:51.834 --> 00:00:54.570
- I was thinking back what
I was doing at those ages.

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:55.410
I was like, "Yeah...

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.527
Yeah, I was watching
'That's So Raven' at 12."

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:01.980
- Oh, my goodness. I have
so many questions now.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.860
Did you ever feel like
you were already different

00:01:04.860 --> 00:01:08.220
than other kids, or did people
tell you you were different?

00:01:08.220 --> 00:01:09.510
- I remember back when I was a kid

00:01:09.510 --> 00:01:10.980
and I would go to my friend's house,

00:01:10.980 --> 00:01:12.420
I'd be like, "Hmm, what's a fun activity?

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:13.650
We should try writing some code.

00:01:13.650 --> 00:01:14.850
I should teach you how to code."

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.590
It took me a while to realize
that that's not something

00:01:16.590 --> 00:01:18.960
that people are generally
as interested in as I am.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:22.230
- I think I did web in
high school and coding.

00:01:22.230 --> 00:01:25.680
Oh my, goodness. I was so
frustrated with all the carets.

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:27.107
It was like, "JavaScripts, no bueno."

00:01:27.107 --> 00:01:29.610
- A long time ago I took coding classes,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:32.760
but I guess I just dropped out
because it was really boring.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:33.960
- And how old are you, Chelsea?

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:38.760
- I'm 11 years old, not to be
called a genius. Too young.

00:01:38.760 --> 00:01:41.730
- How do you personally
define intelligence,

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.590
and do you think that it
can truly be quantified?

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.010
- Intelligence is really hard,
if not basically impossible

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:48.450
to quantify into one metric.

00:01:48.450 --> 00:01:49.830
Take IQ scores as an example.

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:54.360
They are still used as this
way of making people feel

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:57.630
inferior or superior in a way
that really doesn't matter.

00:01:57.630 --> 00:01:59.550
- I work in a Title I low-income school

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.800
and it never paints the full
picture of what these kids

00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.870
are capable of because it
doesn't take into account,

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.010
did they get breakfast that day?

00:02:05.010 --> 00:02:07.320
So I really appreciate your
acknowledgement of the fact

00:02:07.320 --> 00:02:10.230
that a lot of these metrics
that we use can have a lot

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:11.880
of neg negative consequences.

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:12.713
- Absolutely.

00:02:12.713 --> 00:02:15.870
- Do you think your childhood
was taken away from you?

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:18.090
- I feel like I've been given so much more

00:02:18.090 --> 00:02:19.769
that so many other
people don't get to have.

00:02:19.769 --> 00:02:23.190
It's... Talking to like any
one of my other friends,

00:02:23.190 --> 00:02:27.270
it's like all of them say
that they would love to be

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.460
in the position that I'm
in, to be totally honest.

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:31.650
And that whole thing is
something I don't regret.

00:02:31.650 --> 00:02:33.750
And as a matter of fact,
what I got in return

00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:35.250
is so much more.

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:36.480
I remember this one trip that I had,

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:38.460
I traveled to like five different
continents from Toronto,

00:02:38.460 --> 00:02:41.520
which is where I live,
to Germany, to India,

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:43.650
to The Bahamas, to Australia
and back in a week.

00:02:43.650 --> 00:02:45.450
And I got to see different
students at different schools

00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.190
in all these different
places, and I got to interact

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:49.260
with them, and I got to
answer their questions.

00:02:49.260 --> 00:02:51.090
And all these experiences are something

00:02:51.090 --> 00:02:53.790
that I just would never
trade for anything else.

00:02:53.790 --> 00:02:57.180
- This might be kind
of an awkward question,

00:02:57.180 --> 00:02:59.340
so brace yourself.

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:01.860
What's dating like for you?

00:03:01.860 --> 00:03:03.480
- I wouldn't know.

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:06.090
Unfortunately, that's not
something I've really done yet.

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.650
It's not something I've,

00:03:07.650 --> 00:03:10.550
for lack of a better way of
phrasing it, had the time for.

00:03:11.700 --> 00:03:14.280
So I guess we'll find
out in a couple years,

00:03:14.280 --> 00:03:16.770
maybe we'll have an update. (chuckles)

00:03:16.770 --> 00:03:17.603
- Have a wonderful night.

00:03:17.603 --> 00:03:18.510
- Thank you. You, too.

00:03:18.510 --> 00:03:21.780
Oh and also, if you wanna... One second.

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:27.090
If you ever wanted to
connect at some point.

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:29.883
- As a youth or whatever,
do you party and stuff?

00:03:30.960 --> 00:03:34.200
- It's something that my friends
have tried to get me to do.

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:35.880
It's something that I have
the opportunity to do.

00:03:35.880 --> 00:03:37.650
It's not something that I enjoy very much.

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:41.190
A couple of days ago when I
turned 19, my friends threw me

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:42.990
a surprise birthday party,
which was really fun.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:44.632
They got me a cake and on the cake,

00:03:44.632 --> 00:03:48.630
they had "Happy birthday" written out,

00:03:48.630 --> 00:03:50.610
but printed in Rust code.

00:03:50.610 --> 00:03:52.830
And so... (chuckles)

00:03:52.830 --> 00:03:56.130
- What is the most annoying
misconception people have

00:03:56.130 --> 00:03:59.190
about being child genius?

00:03:59.190 --> 00:04:02.460
- They assume that I don't
do anything "for fun,"

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.590
or I lack like social interaction,

00:04:04.590 --> 00:04:05.910
or like I don't have
friends because of it.

00:04:05.910 --> 00:04:07.380
I didn't really regret missing out

00:04:07.380 --> 00:04:09.690
on whatever I did miss
out on to begin with.

00:04:09.690 --> 00:04:12.450
- Well, what do you like to do
for fun outside of your job?

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:13.290
- I'd say I love to code.

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:16.590
I do it as a recreational
activity, and suddenly people

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:18.525
are like, "Sure, but
what do you do for fun?"

00:04:18.525 --> 00:04:21.570
(both chuckle)

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.633
I love movies and TV shows.

00:04:23.633 --> 00:04:26.280
I mean, I remember traditionally SpongeBob

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.918
is still my favorite
TV show, realistically.

00:04:27.918 --> 00:04:30.120
- That's so awesome. (chuckles)

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:32.580
- Do you think that robots will take over?

00:04:32.580 --> 00:04:34.650
- Definitely not. Robots are
not taking over the world.

00:04:34.650 --> 00:04:37.250
That is one of the
misconceptions I love to address.

00:04:38.514 --> 00:04:39.810
- Good, good. Yeah.

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:41.010
Whew.

00:04:41.010 --> 00:04:43.080
- I don't blame anybody for
having those misconceptions.

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:44.820
- Do you think you have strict parents?

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.180
- No, because if you look at
the things they've let me do,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:49.740
it's incredibly unconventional.

00:04:49.740 --> 00:04:52.560
My entire family is so supportive
of the stuff that I do.

00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.540
- Parental support is so important.

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:55.373
- We'll trust you enough.

00:04:55.373 --> 00:04:57.690
We'll put in the effort to
let you be homeschooled.

00:04:57.690 --> 00:04:59.340
And then when I wanted
to start working at IBM,

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:00.690
they're like, "You know what, sure.

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:03.450
Maybe don't go to university,"
because so many of my mentors

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:05.760
were like, "You wouldn't
really gain much from it."

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:08.280
And now it's really fascinating
because I'm like a professor

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:11.490
at the University of Winnipeg
and at a university in Tokyo,

00:05:11.490 --> 00:05:13.080
and I get to teach artificial intelligence

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:14.100
and machine-learning, so--

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.297
- College is overrated.

00:05:15.297 --> 00:05:16.890
(both laugh)

00:05:16.890 --> 00:05:17.880
- Do you always assume

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:19.680
that you're the smartest
person in the room?

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:20.513
- Definitely not.

00:05:20.513 --> 00:05:22.080
I actually currently also work at IBM,

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:24.270
and a lot of the time I'll
be in meetings with people

00:05:24.270 --> 00:05:26.520
who have been working
on products for decades.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:29.310
- I think it's honestly
probably good that you don't.

00:05:29.310 --> 00:05:31.530
- Do people question your intelligence

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:32.610
because you're so young?

00:05:32.610 --> 00:05:33.570
- There's still going to be some people

00:05:33.570 --> 00:05:35.026
that at first are like, "Wait, what?

00:05:35.026 --> 00:05:37.530
Why? Why are we hiring a 17 year old?"

00:05:37.530 --> 00:05:40.200
I remember some folks that
had worked with me before

00:05:40.200 --> 00:05:41.437
were sort of fighting for the whole,

00:05:41.437 --> 00:05:43.230
"No, he does know what
he is talking about.

00:05:43.230 --> 00:05:44.640
He does have that expertise."

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.950
- Do you think that your
label as a genius means

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.150
that you contribute more to society?

00:05:48.150 --> 00:05:49.800
- I think we all contribute
in different ways.

00:05:49.800 --> 00:05:51.930
It depends on your
definition of an impact,

00:05:51.930 --> 00:05:53.280
or what value really is to you.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:54.810
- Please, teach me how to code.

00:05:54.810 --> 00:05:56.010
- Honestly, absolutely.

00:05:56.010 --> 00:05:57.240
I help my friends out all the the time.

00:05:57.240 --> 00:05:58.620
So honestly, I'm happy to do so.

00:05:58.620 --> 00:05:59.850
Feel free to reach out to me.

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.910
- That teacher I had last
time did not work for me.

00:06:02.910 --> 00:06:04.830
- Since you started working so young,

00:06:04.830 --> 00:06:07.350
you could retire really
young, potentially.

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:09.090
Is this something you look forward to?

00:06:09.090 --> 00:06:11.130
- And this is actually a
question that one of my friends

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:11.963
asked me a little while ago.

00:06:11.963 --> 00:06:14.130
She said, "I'm pretty sure
if we gave you two options,

00:06:14.130 --> 00:06:15.990
it's either you continue
doing what you normally do

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.390
for a month or you get to
go on a really fun vacation,

00:06:18.390 --> 00:06:20.340
but there's no tech at all for that month.

00:06:20.340 --> 00:06:21.780
You'd probably just
keep doing what you do."

00:06:21.780 --> 00:06:23.040
And I was like, "Yeah, absolutely."

00:06:23.040 --> 00:06:24.720
I'm still gonna keep doing
the stuff that I love to do,

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:26.370
because it's honestly really fun for me.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:27.750
I want to keep doing it.

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:29.850
- Are there any other
facets of your existence

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:31.650
you feel don't get as much attention?

00:06:31.650 --> 00:06:33.450
- One personality trait that I have

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.700
that maybe people don't realize,

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.100
and I think that's what
leads to quite a few

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:39.840
of the questions that
they ask me, is the fact

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:42.693
that I'm naturally just
a really curious person.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.730
If I am given a topic
on pretty much anything,

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:50.670
I love delving deep into it
regardless of what it's about.

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:54.150
And same thing with tech, and
because I'm also so passionate

00:06:54.150 --> 00:06:56.280
about it along with
that natural curiosity,

00:06:56.280 --> 00:06:58.860
I end up gaining a lot
of experience doing it.

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.810
- Are you set for life financially,

00:07:00.810 --> 00:07:03.024
because you started
working so young in tech?

00:07:03.024 --> 00:07:04.890
(both chuckle)

00:07:04.890 --> 00:07:07.200
- You can kind of tell which
software developers are

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.580
in the field just for money.

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:09.990
But anyway, that's a different thing.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:12.480
I've never done the work
that I do for the money.

00:07:12.480 --> 00:07:14.310
The vast majority of my apps are free.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.320
Almost all the code that
I write is open source,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.030
except for obviously proprietary stuff

00:07:18.030 --> 00:07:19.470
for a company, like at IBM.

00:07:19.470 --> 00:07:23.040
And so I've always done it
with the goal and the ambition

00:07:23.040 --> 00:07:25.140
of helping other people as much as I can.

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:28.110
Some people say that Google
is paying me somewhere

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:29.310
between a couple thousand

00:07:29.310 --> 00:07:31.680
to a couple million dollars a month.

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:33.690
Some people say that
I'm already worth, like,

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:35.287
I think I saw the
largest estimate was like

00:07:35.287 --> 00:07:37.440
$230 something million.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:39.150
I don't know where they're
getting these numbers from.

00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:41.370
All I can say is that they're
definitely not accurate.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:43.590
Google does not pay me.
I do not work for them.

00:07:43.590 --> 00:07:46.440
- Do you think that your
work will be world-changing?

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.810
- You can change the world
just by having an impact

00:07:48.810 --> 00:07:49.860
on a few people.

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:51.600
More specifically,
actually, one of my goals

00:07:51.600 --> 00:07:54.210
is to reach out to and help
at least a hundred thousand

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.730
aspiring beginners, really
help them learn how to code,

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.080
help them innovate with code.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:07:59.910
And so that definitely is something

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:00.780
that I wanna work towards.

00:08:00.780 --> 00:08:01.650
That's what I'm striving for.

00:08:01.650 --> 00:08:03.780
- I'm very impressed by you.

00:08:03.780 --> 00:08:07.560
And we should be friends,
to be honest. (laughs)

00:08:07.560 --> 00:08:10.257
- Hey, nice to meet you.
- Josh. Nice to meet you.

00:08:10.257 --> 00:08:11.400
- Not what I had in my mind.

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:12.509
(Tanmay laughs)

00:08:12.509 --> 00:08:13.342
- [Tanmay] what did you have in mind?

00:08:13.342 --> 00:08:16.140
- Maybe like a tie and like a tuxedo.

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:17.160
- Super funny story.

00:08:17.160 --> 00:08:19.050
Back in kindergarten, I
wanted to use the computer

00:08:19.050 --> 00:08:20.377
in our class, but my teacher was like,

00:08:20.377 --> 00:08:21.450
"It doesn't work. You can't use it."

00:08:21.450 --> 00:08:23.160
And so I was like, "Okay,
can I take a look at it?"

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:23.993
Everything's a mess.

00:08:23.993 --> 00:08:27.120
These are like really old
computers, tons of cables.

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:28.890
Spent a couple minutes and I realized

00:08:28.890 --> 00:08:30.743
that the power cable wasn't plugged in.

00:08:31.830 --> 00:08:33.930
My teacher shouts to my
mom that, "Hey, you're son,

00:08:33.930 --> 00:08:36.430
he's gonna be talented
with tech when he's older."

